pipeline designed to ingest astrophysical data streams securely and
integrate them into the relativistic evolution. This pipeline ensures
data provenance, cryptographic integrity, and numerical stability while
mapping observational inputs into boundary conditions and source terms.

Telemetry sources include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Gaia DR3 astrometric catalogs,
    \item LIGO O4 gravitational strain time series,
    \item auxiliary photometric, spectroscopic, and timing datasets.
\end{itemize}

All incoming data is encapsulated using lattice-based post-quantum
cryptography (PQC) prior to ingestion.

\section{Lattice-Based PQC Encapsulation}

We employ a lattice-based cryptosystem of the form:
\begin{equation}
    C = \mathcal{E}(D, K),
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $D$ is the raw telemetry data,
    \item $K$ is a post-quantum public key,
    \item $C$ is the encrypted ciphertext.
\end{itemize}

Decryption uses:
\begin{equation}
    D = \mathcal{D}(C, K_{\text{priv}}),
\end{equation}
with $K_{\text{priv}}$ stored in a secure enclave.

The cryptosystem is resistant to:
\begin{itemize}
    \item quantum polynomial-time attacks,
    \item lattice reduction heuristics,
    \item side-channel leakage.
\end{itemize}

